  "content": {
    "id": "v2-954e5-p15-practice-slot-7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $3x^3-3x^2+4x+6=0$ have roots $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "v2-954e5-p15-practice-slot-7-slot-7-q-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the value of $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-\\dfrac53$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma&=-\\frac{-3}{3}=1\\\\\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma&=\\frac43\\\\\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2&=(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)^2-2(\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma)\\\\&=1^2-2\\left(\\frac43\\right)\\\\&=-\\frac53\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-954e5-p15-practice-slot-7-slot-7-q-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, explain why the graph of $y=3x^3-3x^2+4x+6$ cannot have 3 $x$-intercepts.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If all three roots were real, the sum of their squares would be non-negative. Since the sum is $-\\frac53$, the roots cannot all be real, so the graph cannot have 3 $x$-intercepts.",
          "worked": "A cubic has three roots, counting multiplicity. If all three were real, $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2\\geq0$. Part a gives $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2=-\\dfrac53$, so the roots cannot all be real. Hence the graph cannot have 3 $x$-intercepts.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
